※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⎕syscall(NUMBER, ARG...)` asks the kernel for something.  The number and the
※ arguments are the kernel's and not the language's: nothing in the compiler
※ knows what call one is, and a program that asks for it is asking the system.
※
※ **What comes back is what the kernel put in the register it answers in** -- an
※ `i64`, negative where it refused, which is the kernel's own convention and not
※ one invented here.  Reading an error out of it is the caller's business, and
※ will be the `std` module's.
※
※ **Everything given is a machine word.**  A number of any width is widened by
※ its own signedness and a reference is read as the address it is; anything else
※ is refused (4572), there being no register a value of it would go in.  Doing
※ that here rather than in three back ends is what keeps each of them to one
※ shape: by the time one sees a request to the kernel, everything in it is the
※ width of a register.
※
※ **Asking the kernel changes what outlives the call**, so a function that does
※ it says `impure`, which is the rule everything else of that sort follows.
※
※ **The numbers are the architecture's, and `⎕sc@NAME` is how one is named**:
※ `write` is 1 on x86-64 and 64 on the other two, and one source says `⎕sc@write`
※ and means whichever it is being built for.  The compiler holds the table
※ because the compiler is what knows the architecture; a name it has no number
※ for is refused (4574), and one no architecture has is refused differently
※ (4575), so a mistyped call and a call that is simply not there read apart.
※
※ **A name the compiler provides may carry a key**, written after `@`, and only
※ such a name may: `a@b` is still two things with nothing between them, and `@[`
※ still begins an attribute list.
※
※ Compare: **Zig**, whose `std.os.linux.syscall1` and the rest are the same
※ thing with the number named; **Go**, whose `syscall.Syscall` takes three
※ machine words and answers two; **C**, where `syscall(2)` is a variadic
※ function of the library and not of the language; **Rust**, which has no such
※ thing in its standard library at all and reaches for a crate.

※ A number no kernel has a call for, which every one of the three refuses.
let NO_SUCH_CALL: i64 = 0x4000i64

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ one source, three architectures: `⎕sc@write` is 1 here and 64 there
    let message: u8⟦6⟧ = ⟦104u8, 101u8, 108u8, 108u8, 111u8, 10u8⟧ in ⎕static
    let wrote: i64 = ⎕syscall(⎕sc@write, 1i64, &message⟦0⟧, 6i64)
    ※ the number alone
    let bare: i64 = ⎕syscall(NO_SUCH_CALL)
    ※ and with every argument the kernel takes, one of them an address and one
    ※ of them narrower than a register
    let full: i64 = ⎕syscall(NO_SUCH_CALL, 1i64, &message⟦0⟧, 6u8,
                             ⁻1i64, 0i64, 0i64)
    if wrote ≠ 6i64:
        1u6
    elif bare ≥ 0i64:
        2u6
    elif full ≥ 0i64:
        3u6
    else:
        0u6
